WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.647
CASE lets us create derived values based on rules.

00:00:03.647 --> 00:00:07.522
You can think of it as a decision tree written inside SQL.

00:00:07.522 --> 00:00:11.857
PostgreSQL evaluates the WHEN branches from top to bottom.

00:00:11.857 --> 00:00:19.342
As soon as one condition is true, CASE returns that branch's result and stops checking the later branches

00:00:19.342 --> 00:00:22.197
for that row. That makes branch order part of the logic.

00:00:22.197 --> 00:00:24.997
Suppose we are labeling support tickets.

00:00:24.997 --> 00:00:29.417
If the first branch says response time greater than one hour equals Delayed,

00:00:29.417 --> 00:00:36.337
and a later branch says response time greater than twenty-four hours equals Critical,

00:00:36.337 --> 00:00:41.722
the Critical branch will never run because every ticket above twenty-four hours already satisfies the broader

00:00:41.722 --> 00:00:45.412
one-hour condition.

00:00:45.412 --> 00:00:51.292
The safer order is usually specific before broad, or highest priority before lower priority.

00:00:51.292 --> 00:00:51.642
CASE can also include ELSE.

00:00:51.642 --> 00:00:57.272
ELSE gives a default result when no WHEN condition matches.

00:00:57.272 --> 00:00:59.897
If ELSE is omitted, PostgreSQL returns NULL.

00:00:59.897 --> 00:01:07.012
Before writing a CASE expression, I recommend listing the categories in plain language and checking for

00:01:07.012 --> 00:01:10.832
overlap. Ask: can one row satisfy more than one branch?

00:01:10.832 --> 00:01:11.907
If yes, which result should win?

00:01:11.907 --> 00:01:19.392
CASE is powerful because it allows business logic to become visible directly in a query.

00:01:19.392 --> 00:01:25.922
But that also means poorly ordered branches can encode the wrong policy while still producing valid-looking

00:01:25.922 --> 00:01:26.297
output.
